  "limitations": [
    "Future weather is persistence (same hour on the most recent observed day), not a real NWP feed, so error during weather events is understated.",
    "capacity_mw is a p99-of-load proxy: 'overload' means unusually high for this zone, not a verified thermal emergency. Absolute risk values inherit that.",
    "The live /risk endpoint scores one historical snapshot matched to the current weekday+hour; this replay scores every hour in sequence, so it measures the model, not the snapshot-selection rule.",
    "Labels derive from the same load series the models consume, so a sensor outage or curtailment that suppressed measured load would read as 'no overload'.",
    "Overload onsets cluster at each zone's daily peak hour, so a long 'lead time' can come from reproducing the climatological peak shape rather than new information. Read it with the h+6 warning-rule precision gate, which scores the rule over all origins \u2014 including the hours where it cries wolf.",
    "The newest hour this replay can score is 26 h behind wall clock. Load arrives from ENTSO-E as it is republished, and NASA POWER only exposes an hour roughly two days after it happens; a row missing any weather input is dropped rather than fed to the model with NaNs, because LightGBM silently predicts through missing features. So the served origin is the last *fully observed* hour, not the current one. A near-real-time provider was measured as a substitute and rejected for now: it tracks NASA on temperature (r=0.92, bias +0.4 degC) but not on wind (1.8 vs 2.9 m/s, r=0.62), and splicing it into the tail would score the forecast against a feature distribution the model was never trained on."
  ],
